The SM7150 lite CSID is not where mainline thinks it is

Mainline puts the SM7150 in the same branch as the SM8250 (Titan 480) to compute
the CSID address, which on both lives INSIDE the VFE region. For the two normal
CSIDs the number matches and is verified:

    vfe0  0x0acaf000 + 0x4000 = 0x0acb3000 = cam_csid0    (Android DT)
    vfe1  0x0acb6000 + 0x4000 = 0x0acba000 = cam_csid1

But for the LITE one it uses 0x200, which is the Titan 480 one, and on the
SM7150 it is 0x4000 like the others:

    vfe_lite  0x0acc4000 + 0x200  = 0x0acc4200   <- what mainline does
    cam_csid_lite0                = 0x0acc8000   <- what Android says

that is, csid2 points INSIDE the lite VFE. It answers, because it is a valid
register region, but it is not the CSID: anything written to it ends up in the
lite VFE registers.

The SM7150 branch is split from the SM8250 one instead of touching the constant,
because 0x200 is still correct for the real Titan 480.

Found by comparing the surya's own Android device tree with the mainline one,
while looking for why no sensor delivers a single symbol to the CSID on this
SoC.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
@@ -1114,7 +1114,26 @@
 
 	/* Memory */
 
-	if (camss->res->version == CAMSS_8250 || camss->res->version == CAMSS_7150) {
+	if (camss->res->version == CAMSS_7150) {
+		/*
+		 * En el SM7150 el CSID lite NO esta a 0x200 del VFE lite, sino
+		 * a 0x4000, igual que los otros dos. Del device tree de Android
+		 * del propio movil:
+		 *
+		 *     vfe_lite        0x0acc4000   (mainline, reg-names)
+		 *     cam_csid_lite0  0x0acc8000   (Android, qcom,csid_lite@)
+		 *
+		 * o sea 0x4000 de diferencia, no los 0x200 del Titan 480. Con
+		 * el 0x200 el csid2 apunta DENTRO del VFE lite: contesta,
+		 * porque es una region valida, pero no es el CSID.
+		 *
+		 * Los otros dos si cuadran con el 0x4000 de siempre:
+		 *     vfe0 0x0acaf000 + 0x4000 = 0x0acb3000 = cam_csid0
+		 *     vfe1 0x0acb6000 + 0x4000 = 0x0acba000 = cam_csid1
+		 */
+		csid->base = csid->res->parent_dev_ops->get_base_address(camss, id)
+			+ VFE_480_CSID_OFFSET;
+	} else if (camss->res->version == CAMSS_8250) {
 		/* for titan 480, CSID registers are inside the VFE region,
 		 * between the VFE "top" and "bus" registers. this requires
 		 * VFE to be initialized before CSID
